## 1. Objectives

SolarAudit supports the full lifecycle of a solar installation assessment, from field data collection to a signed-off engineering report.

```text
Client
  │
  ▼
Project
  │
  ▼
Site / Load Audit
  │
  ▼
Energy Analysis
  │
  ├── Load Calculation
  ├── Daily Energy
  ├── Peak / Connected Load
  └── Essential Load
  │
  ▼
System Sizing
  │
  ├── Solar PV
  ├── Battery
  ├── Inverter
  ├── Charge Controller
  ├── Cable
  └── Protection
  │
  ▼
Engineering Validation
  │
  ▼
Bill of Materials
  │
  ▼
Engineering Report
```

---

## 2. Engineering Engines

The calculation layer is divided into independent, composable domains:

```text
Engineering Engine
│
├── load
├── energy
├── solar
├── battery
├── inverter
├── cable
├── voltage-drop
├── charge-controller
├── protection
├── bom
├── costing
├── report
├── units
├── constants
└── validation
```

Every engine exposes an explicit contract:

- **Inputs** — typed, validated
- **Assumptions** — documented, versioned
- **Calculations** — pure, side-effect-free
- **Outputs** — structured result object
- **Warnings / Errors** — non-throwing, collected

---

## 3. API Surface

Each engine is reachable through a dedicated endpoint:

```text
POST /api/load/calculate
POST /api/energy/analyze
POST /api/solar/size
POST /api/battery/size
POST /api/inverter/size
POST /api/cable/size
POST /api/protection/size
```

The API layer is intentionally thin — it maps HTTP requests to engine calls and returns results. No engineering logic lives here.

```text
                    REST API
                       │
        ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
        │              │              │
        ▼              ▼              ▼
      Load           Solar         Battery
     Engine          Engine         Engine
        │              │              │
        └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                       │
                 Validation
                       │
                       ▼
                  Calculation
                       │
                       ▼
                    Result
```

---

## 4. Load Audit

The load audit is the foundation of every sizing decision.

**Each load item captures:**

```text
Load Name
Quantity
Rated Power
Operating Hours
Usage Pattern
Load Type
Power Factor
Diversity Factor
Essential / Non-essential
```

**Example:**

```text
Television
Quantity:     1
Power:        120 W
Hours/day:    5
Essential:    Yes
```

**The load engine determines:**

```text
Connected Load
Running Load
Diversified Load
Daily Energy
Essential Load
Peak Demand
```

---

## 5. Energy Analysis

The energy engine aggregates loads into daily system requirements.

**Typical outputs:**

```text
Total connected load
Diversified load
Daily energy consumption
Essential daily energy
Peak demand
Average operating load
Estimated battery energy requirement
Estimated PV energy requirement
```

Every result preserves its underlying assumptions so any number can be traced back to its inputs.

---

## 6. Solar PV Sizing

The solar engine sizes the PV array from the daily energy requirement.

**Inputs:**

```text
Daily energy requirement
Peak sun hours
System efficiency
Temperature / environmental derating
Design margin
Panel rated power
Panel operating voltage
Panel current
Series panel count
Parallel string count
```

**Flow:**

```text
Daily Energy
     │
     ▼
PV Energy Requirement
     │
     ▼
PV Array Power
     │
     ▼
Panel Selection
     │
     ├── Series
     └── Parallel
     │
     ▼
Final PV Array
```

The result reports both the **calculated requirement** and the **selected practical configuration**.

---

## 7. Battery Sizing

The battery engine sizes storage based on autonomy and daily energy.

**Inputs:**

```text
Daily energy
Backup autonomy
System voltage
Depth of discharge
Battery efficiency
Temperature derating
Design margin
Battery capacity
Battery quantity
Series strings
Parallel strings
```

**Supported system voltages:**

```text
12 V    24 V    36 V    48 V
```

**Example architecture:**

```text
             BATTERY BANK
                  │
       ┌──────────┴──────────┐
       │                     │
   Series String 1       Series String 2
       │                     │
    4 × 12 V             4 × 12 V
       │                     │
       └──────────┬──────────┘
                  │
                48 V
```

**The engine distinguishes:**

```text
Calculated capacity
Selected battery capacity
Required series count
Required parallel count
Actual installed capacity
```

---

## 8. Inverter Sizing

The inverter engine determines the required rating for both continuous and surge duty.

**Inputs:**

```text
Peak load
Continuous load
Surge load
Motor starting requirements
Design margin
Output voltage
Output frequency
System voltage
```

**Outputs:**

```text
Minimum inverter rating
Recommended inverter rating
Required surge capability
DC input current
Operating voltage
```

---

## 9. Cable Sizing

The cable engine evaluates conductor adequacy for every circuit in the system.

**Inputs:**

```text
Load current
Cable length
Conductor material
System voltage
Allowable voltage drop
Cable resistance
Cable cross-sectional area
Installation conditions
Temperature
```

**Outputs:**

```text
Calculated minimum cable size
Selected cable size
Estimated voltage drop
Voltage-drop percentage
Current carrying requirement
```

**Applied independently to:**

```text
Battery cables
PV cables
Inverter cables
AC output cables
Charge-controller cables
Interconnects
```

---

## 10. Voltage Drop

Voltage drop is a standalone service so it can be reused by any engine.

```text
Cable Parameters
      │
      ▼
Resistance
      │
      ▼
Current
      │
      ▼
Voltage Drop
      │
      ▼
Voltage Drop %
```

The result reports both **absolute** and **percentage** voltage drop.

---

## 11. Charge Controller Sizing

The controller engine selects an appropriate charge controller.

**Inputs:**

```text
PV array power
Battery voltage
PV operating voltage
PV current
Charging current
Controller efficiency
Design margin
Controller technology (MPPT / PWM)
```

**Outputs:**

```text
Required charging current
Minimum controller rating
Recommended controller rating
PV voltage compatibility
PV current compatibility
```

---

## 12. Protection Sizing

Protection is treated as a first-class engineering subsystem, not an afterthought.

**Coverage:**

```text
Battery fuse / breaker
PV fuse / breaker
DC isolator
AC breaker
Surge protection (SPD)
Over-current protection
Short-circuit protection
```

**Selection criteria:**

```text
Operating current
Expected fault current
System voltage
Device interrupt rating
Continuous-duty requirements
Design margin
```

---

## 13. Bill of Materials

The BOM engine converts engineering selections into a structured project material list.

**Categories:**

```text
PV modules
Batteries
Inverter
Charge controller
DC breakers
AC breakers
Fuses
SPDs
Cables
Connectors
Busbars
Enclosures
Mounting hardware
Earthing materials
Monitoring equipment
```

**Each BOM item supports:**

```text
Category
Description
Manufacturer
Part number
Specification
Quantity
Unit cost
Total cost
Supplier
Alternative
Engineering notes
```

---

## 14. Costing

Costing converts the BOM into project-level financial information.

```text
Component Cost
      │
      ▼
Quantity
      │
      ▼
Extended Cost
      │
      ▼
Subtotal
      │
      ├── Installation
      ├── Transport
      ├── Engineering
      └── Other Costs
      │
      ▼
Project Total
```

**Costing is strictly separate from engineering.** A price change must never affect a sizing result.

---

## 15. Engineering Validation

Every calculation passes through validation before it is accepted.

**Validation checks:**

```text
Required fields
Numeric ranges
Electrical limits
System-voltage compatibility
Component limits
Design margins
Unit consistency
Invalid configurations
Missing dependencies
```

```text
PV Array
   │
   ▼
Voltage Check
   │
   ├── PASS
   └── FAIL
        │
        ▼
   Validation Error
```

Validation never throws — it returns structured warnings and errors so the caller decides how to react.

---

